WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.268
-Our next guest
is a talented actress

00:00:02.268 --> 00:00:03.936
you know from movies
like "Scream 3,"

00:00:03.936 --> 00:00:05.404
"Party Girl,"
and "Best in Show."

00:00:05.404 --> 00:00:08.141
She stars in "Beau Is Afraid,"
which is in theaters now.

00:00:08.141 --> 00:00:09.141
Let's take a look.

00:00:09.141 --> 00:00:11.578
-I can't believe it's you.

00:00:12.545 --> 00:00:14.547
[Music plays over radio]

00:00:14.547 --> 00:00:16.783
-I can't believe it's you.

00:00:18.951 --> 00:00:21.554
-Do I look different?

00:00:21.554 --> 00:00:23.556
You recognize me?

00:00:23.556 --> 00:00:24.791
I'm old now.

00:00:26.425 --> 00:00:27.527
-You're the same.

00:00:29.896 --> 00:00:31.097
-No, I'm not.

00:00:32.065 --> 00:00:33.099
-You are.

00:00:34.067 --> 00:00:35.568
-Well, you're the same, too.

00:00:35.568 --> 00:00:37.737
Except for your body
and your face.

00:00:39.672 --> 00:00:40.940
-Please welcome to the show.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:42.608
Parker Posey, everyone.

00:00:42.608 --> 00:01:02.027 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:02.027 --> 00:01:03.562
Hi!
-Hi!

00:01:03.562 --> 00:01:05.898
-Fancy white boots?
Don't mind if I do.

00:01:05.898 --> 00:01:07.099
-Thank you!
This is...

00:01:07.099 --> 00:01:09.435
I'm giving you Agnes Moorehead
from "Bewitched."

00:01:09.435 --> 00:01:10.903
-[ Chuckles ]
Oh, thank you for that.

00:01:10.903 --> 00:01:12.038
-Yeah, you're welcome.
-Thank you very much.

00:01:12.038 --> 00:01:13.406
It's so lovely
to have you here.

00:01:13.406 --> 00:01:15.875
-I know. I can't believe
I've never been on your show.

00:01:15.875 --> 00:01:17.176
-Because we've been
lucky enough to run

00:01:17.176 --> 00:01:18.278
into each over the years.

00:01:18.278 --> 00:01:19.812
-That's right.
-You were --

00:01:19.812 --> 00:01:22.581
So we were
at a wedding together.

00:01:22.581 --> 00:01:24.884
Our friend Chris Kattan.
-Chris Kattan at Yosemite.

00:01:24.884 --> 00:01:26.052
-It was in Yosemite.

00:01:26.052 --> 00:01:29.189
-Forest fires like big fires,
very smokey.

00:01:29.189 --> 00:01:31.524
-Yeah, choked -- It was the
Smokiest wedding I've been at.

00:01:31.524 --> 00:01:33.126
-Yes. Yes, [Laughing]
it was very smokey.

00:01:33.126 --> 00:01:34.460
-It was very smokey.
But that's where I met...

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:35.761
-I think it was staining
people's,

00:01:35.761 --> 00:01:36.763
you know,
clothes and stuff.

00:01:36.763 --> 00:01:38.264
-Yes. When it was over,

00:01:38.264 --> 00:01:39.565
we all looked like
chimney sweeps.

00:01:39.565 --> 00:01:41.100
-[Chuckles]
That's right.

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:42.468
-You were the best man
at that wedding.

00:01:42.468 --> 00:01:44.270
-That's right.
-You spoke on behalf of Chris.

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:45.471
-That's right.

00:01:45.471 --> 00:01:47.006
-And how -- Well, did you enjoy
that experience?

00:01:47.006 --> 00:01:48.174
-I did.

00:01:48.174 --> 00:01:49.175
-You did a wonderful speech.
-I did.

00:01:49.175 --> 00:01:50.410
-We had a -- Thank you.

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:52.278
And it was in front
of a lot of comedians.

00:01:52.278 --> 00:01:53.579
-There were a lot of comedians.
Yeah. That raises the stakes.

00:01:53.579 --> 00:01:55.314
-Jennifer Coolidge.
-Jennifer Coolidge was there.

00:01:55.314 --> 00:01:57.249
-Brooke Shields was there, too.
-Brook Shields was there.

00:01:57.249 --> 00:01:58.884
It was a real A-list affair.
Yeah.

00:01:58.884 --> 00:01:59.885
[Audience laughs]

00:01:59.885 --> 00:02:02.021
-Have you seen her, doc?

00:02:02.021 --> 00:02:03.556
- I have seen "Pretty Baby."
-On Hulu?

00:02:03.556 --> 00:02:05.457
-The Brook -- It was wonderful.
-Oh, my God. It was so good.

00:02:05.457 --> 00:02:06.792
-It was great.
-I watched it over the weekend.

00:02:06.792 --> 00:02:08.594
-Here we go, great. Look at us.
-Yeah. Yeah, I know.

00:02:08.594 --> 00:02:10.262
[Audience laughs]

00:02:10.262 --> 00:02:12.465
-You've been a New Yorker
since the early '90s, correct?

00:02:12.465 --> 00:02:13.533
-That's right. Yeah.

00:02:13.533 --> 00:02:15.001
-How do you feel
like New York is doing?

00:02:15.001 --> 00:02:16.369
Because you've stayed --
you've stayed the course?

00:02:16.369 --> 00:02:17.603
-I know. You know,

00:02:17.603 --> 00:02:20.673
I haven't lived in the city
in like six years.

00:02:20.673 --> 00:02:24.210
I've been back and forth,
and I'm a '90s girl.

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:26.546
I'm really happy the '90s
are back now.

00:02:26.546 --> 00:02:27.547
-Okay.

00:02:27.547 --> 00:02:29.782
People seem to be,
like, having fun.

00:02:29.782 --> 00:02:31.650
-Yeah.
-And, like, I was in Atlanta.

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:32.752
I was working in Atlanta.

00:02:32.752 --> 00:02:34.553
and I had a Lyft driver
and I called her.

00:02:34.553 --> 00:02:36.822
And she had her hair
all curled up.

00:02:36.822 --> 00:02:38.590
She was going out that night.

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:40.993
Hair in curlers,
did not care

00:02:40.993 --> 00:02:43.095
because she was going out
to have a good time later.

00:02:43.095 --> 00:02:44.463
-Great.
-I love that.

00:02:44.463 --> 00:02:47.367
-I think I worked with
some young people on the play

00:02:47.367 --> 00:02:49.102
that I just did,
and they're...

00:02:49.102 --> 00:02:50.236
-And young people
are happy to be here?

00:02:50.236 --> 00:02:51.604
-They're happy to be here.
-That's great.

00:02:51.604 --> 00:02:55.575
-And they like the vibe.
And I'm just -- I'm --

00:02:55.575 --> 00:02:57.443
You know, it's the people
that make New York.

00:02:57.443 --> 00:02:59.478
It's like walking
on the sidewalks.

00:02:59.478 --> 00:03:02.615
It's saying hi to the dogs.
It's looking in storefronts.

00:03:02.615 --> 00:03:05.284
It's connecting to everybody
and writing with that energy

00:03:05.284 --> 00:03:06.853
and being spontaneous.

00:03:06.853 --> 00:03:08.187
And that's why you live
in New York.

00:03:08.187 --> 00:03:09.388
-There you go.

00:03:09.388 --> 00:03:12.725
-I can complain about
how expensive wine is.

00:03:12.725 --> 00:03:14.727
Like a glass of wine
is like $20.

00:03:14.727 --> 00:03:16.629
-Right. By the glass.
-I think that is wrong.

00:03:16.629 --> 00:03:17.930
-Yes.
-I mean, remember...

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:20.499
- There should be -- Adam's
should put a wine cap

00:03:20.499 --> 00:03:22.301
on how much
you can charge per glass.

00:03:22.301 --> 00:03:23.969
-It's like that much, too.
-Yeah.

00:03:23.969 --> 00:03:25.371
-It's that's --
That's not...

00:03:25.371 --> 00:03:26.806
-And then when you bring
your own bottle of wine

00:03:26.806 --> 00:03:28.240
to a restaurant
and pour it under the table,

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:29.241
they give you a hard time.

00:03:29.241 --> 00:03:30.576
-That's right.

00:03:30.576 --> 00:03:32.311
They sure do.
-[Chuckles]

00:03:32.311 --> 00:03:36.448
Hey, so this is -- this
is fantastically a weird film,

00:03:36.448 --> 00:03:37.683
a wonderfully weird film.

00:03:37.683 --> 00:03:39.619
Joaquin Phoenix
and the director, Ari Aster,

00:03:39.619 --> 00:03:41.521
has made some of the...
-He's incredible.

00:03:41.521 --> 00:03:43.556
-He really -- He made
"Hereditary," "Midsommar."

00:03:43.556 --> 00:03:45.824
These are some of the...
-Is everyone an Ari Aster fan?

00:03:45.824 --> 00:03:48.127
[Cheers and applause]

00:03:48.127 --> 00:03:52.698
I mean, we don't support a lot
of auteurs in this country.

00:03:52.698 --> 00:03:55.434
And by that I mean
directors and writers

00:03:55.434 --> 00:03:57.769
making their own film
and having their own vision.

00:03:57.769 --> 00:03:59.705
Quentin Tarantino,
for example.

00:03:59.705 --> 00:04:03.542
So I think Ari is a voice
of his generation,

00:04:03.542 --> 00:04:05.711
a real visionary.

00:04:05.711 --> 00:04:08.547
-And I was just so excited
to be a part of this.

00:04:08.547 --> 00:04:09.915
-It's really fun to sit down
and watch one of his movies,

00:04:09.915 --> 00:04:12.885
and know that it's not going
to go where you think,

00:04:12.885 --> 00:04:14.353
but you are very confident

00:04:14.353 --> 00:04:17.290
that the person who made it
knows exactly why it's -- Yeah.

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:19.359
-He'll elicit
a certain feeling like,

00:04:19.359 --> 00:04:20.626
So that scene you just saw,

00:04:20.626 --> 00:04:22.561
Joaquin and I were laughing
our heads off.

00:04:22.561 --> 00:04:24.330
We just thought
it was so funny.

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:27.200
You know, this guy
is so damaged to the like,

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:29.468
he might as well be in an opera.

00:04:29.468 --> 00:04:31.837
When I saw him
for the first time,

00:04:31.837 --> 00:04:35.107
like, I thought
he was like a worker on set.

00:04:35.107 --> 00:04:37.543
He came in, he was dressed
in these khaki things

00:04:37.543 --> 00:04:39.045
and his head was on him.

00:04:39.045 --> 00:04:40.613
I could -- I didn't even
really see him.

00:04:40.613 --> 00:04:42.948
I just felt him, you know,
And I was like,

00:04:42.948 --> 00:04:46.119
Oh, that poor man.
And he was like, "Hi."

00:04:46.119 --> 00:04:50.423
Like, he'd gain like 35 pounds.
And his head was all scratched.

00:04:50.423 --> 00:04:53.893
And I'm like,
"Oh, my God, how are you?

00:04:53.893 --> 00:04:55.694
"Oh, it's so good to see you."

00:04:55.694 --> 00:04:58.164
You know,
and he's so wonderful

00:04:58.164 --> 00:05:00.432
at playing
all those things at once.

00:05:00.432 --> 00:05:04.903
And so, yeah, Ari got in touch
with my agent, said,

00:05:04.903 --> 00:05:06.739
"Ari Aster wants
to zoom with you."

00:05:06.739 --> 00:05:08.341
And I got on Zoom and...

00:05:08.341 --> 00:05:10.643
-What did he did?
Did you find out? Like what?

00:05:10.643 --> 00:05:12.812
Was there a piece of your work
that Ari had been drawn to?

00:05:12.812 --> 00:05:14.980
-You know, what he really
liked was "Best in Show."

00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:16.148
-Fantastic.
-Yeah.

00:05:16.148 --> 00:05:18.684
-I mean, a great choice.
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:05:18.684 --> 00:05:20.987
-It's also really fun
because, you know, if you --

00:05:20.987 --> 00:05:23.422
If you see a film
like "Hereditary,"

00:05:23.422 --> 00:05:26.258
you don't think the director
has a great sense of humor.

00:05:26.258 --> 00:05:28.294
And so it's really nice.

00:05:28.294 --> 00:05:29.528
-And he does.
-Yeah.

00:05:29.528 --> 00:05:32.064
-Ari has a great --
He's really smart.

00:05:32.064 --> 00:05:35.468
And he's a cinephile.
You know, he's a filmmaker.

00:05:35.468 --> 00:05:38.571
Like, he's --What I love
about this movie

00:05:38.571 --> 00:05:41.940
is that people you will learn
how to talk about movies

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:43.509
by watching this movie

00:05:43.509 --> 00:05:45.877
because people don't really know
how to talk about it.

00:05:45.877 --> 00:05:47.580
And it's kind of divided.

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:49.281
But I just think
it's a wonderful.

00:05:49.281 --> 00:05:54.653
Patti LuPone is the mother
who -- owns his whole life.

00:05:54.653 --> 00:05:57.890
But this like, he makes
this landscape, you know,

00:05:57.890 --> 00:06:01.727
and he, Joaquin,
you can't watch him

00:06:01.727 --> 00:06:05.664
and not just have all
these feelings.

00:06:05.664 --> 00:06:07.600
So we all came in.
I came in at the tail end.

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:09.234
And so did Patty.
-You got -- What are the --

00:06:09.234 --> 00:06:10.836
-We worked two days.

00:06:10.836 --> 00:06:13.639
And, you know, you just hope
for that kind of magic

00:06:13.639 --> 00:06:16.542
when you're working on a film
that just like everything

00:06:16.542 --> 00:06:18.343
that I seem to be doing
at the time

00:06:18.343 --> 00:06:22.714
was had created an atmosphere
around, like, I checked --

00:06:22.714 --> 00:06:25.785
I flew to Atlanta,
and I didn't have my bag,

00:06:25.785 --> 00:06:27.619
and like I was in the wrong,

00:06:27.619 --> 00:06:31.357
I was in the international thing
and I was just like, "Aah!"

00:06:31.357 --> 00:06:33.192
You know I felt like
"Beau is Afraid."

00:06:33.192 --> 00:06:34.360
Was this your fault or did...
Parker was afraid.

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:36.895
-[ Chuckles ]
-No, it was not my fault.

00:06:36.895 --> 00:06:38.230
-Okay.
-But it should stay...

00:06:38.230 --> 00:06:40.065
-Wrong terminal wasn't your
fault, you don't think?

00:06:40.065 --> 00:06:41.300
[Audience laughs]

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:42.902
- Maybe. [Chuckles]
You know, it was, like --

00:06:42.902 --> 00:06:44.803
Because, you know,
I worked all night.

00:06:44.803 --> 00:06:46.271
I worked two nights,

00:06:46.271 --> 00:06:50.042
and then I had to catch
the plane after work.

00:06:50.042 --> 00:06:51.143
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:06:51.143 --> 00:06:52.378
-That gets tricky.
-Yeah, that's hard.

00:06:52.378 --> 00:06:53.612
-One of the things they --
-[Chuckles]

00:06:53.612 --> 00:06:55.047
-One of the things
they put you through as well

00:06:55.047 --> 00:06:57.449
is you had to get a full-body
cast made for your --

00:06:57.449 --> 00:06:58.584
-That's right.

00:06:58.584 --> 00:07:00.219
-For your two days
of work on this film.

00:07:00.219 --> 00:07:02.721
A full-body cast,
I've had a head cast made once

00:07:02.721 --> 00:07:04.156
and it's very claustrophobic.

00:07:04.156 --> 00:07:06.258
How did you take to having
a full-body cast?

00:07:06.258 --> 00:07:08.460
-Yeah, so the --
But it's that material

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:10.163
that when you go to a dentist

00:07:10.163 --> 00:07:12.498
and they put that mold
in your mouth

00:07:12.498 --> 00:07:13.832
and then, like,
[Click].

00:07:13.832 --> 00:07:15.701
I just had that done.

00:07:15.701 --> 00:07:19.371
They covered my entire body
in this for,

00:07:19.371 --> 00:07:20.806
I think a few hours.

00:07:20.806 --> 00:07:22.575
-And are you good
at like sort of...

00:07:22.575 --> 00:07:23.743
-...disassociating?
-Yeah.

00:07:23.743 --> 00:07:25.611
-Absolutely.
That's why I can do what I do.

00:07:25.611 --> 00:07:27.647
-[Chuckles]
-Are you kidding?

00:07:27.647 --> 00:07:30.015
Denial? Yeah.
-[Laughs]

00:07:30.015 --> 00:07:31.984
-Compartmentalizing?
You bet.

00:07:31.984 --> 00:07:33.452
Yeah. Fantastic.

00:07:33.452 --> 00:07:35.421
-Only because,
just commitment to the role

00:07:35.421 --> 00:07:37.122
and because you already
mentioned it.

00:07:37.122 --> 00:07:38.424
Because I also much like Ari,

00:07:38.424 --> 00:07:41.293
a huge fan of your
performance in "Best in Show,"

00:07:41.293 --> 00:07:43.129
and the braces,
you actually --

00:07:43.129 --> 00:07:44.830
Is it true that
you got the braces?

00:07:44.830 --> 00:07:47.299
-Yes.
-Beforehand?

00:07:47.299 --> 00:07:50.836
-Well, okay, so you know
these movies are...

00:07:50.836 --> 00:07:54.039
Chris, allows us to do --
They're all improvised.

00:07:54.039 --> 00:07:55.307
They're unscripted.

00:07:55.307 --> 00:07:56.775
And we were just sitting
at lunch once

00:07:56.775 --> 00:07:59.912
and he's like, "What if you
and..." -- What if you two?

00:07:59.912 --> 00:08:02.781
I was with Hitchcock,
who plays my husband.

00:08:02.781 --> 00:08:04.617
"What if you two had braces?"

00:08:04.617 --> 00:08:08.120
I went, "Oh, we're there.
We're there."

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:11.424
And so Hitchcock
got a retainer

00:08:11.424 --> 00:08:14.160
that he could just put
in his mouth and take out,

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:15.961
and it made him talk
with a lisp.

00:08:15.961 --> 00:08:18.464
But I didn't want to be,
you know,

00:08:18.464 --> 00:08:20.433
his wife talking
with the lisp either.

00:08:20.433 --> 00:08:22.301
And I thought
it would be fun to --

00:08:22.301 --> 00:08:23.402
to get braces.

00:08:23.402 --> 00:08:25.604
I'd had them when I was 12.
-Yeah.

00:08:25.604 --> 00:08:27.573
Not a lot of people
do a second round.

00:08:27.573 --> 00:08:29.107
-No, that's right.

00:08:29.107 --> 00:08:32.578
But people were getting braces,
as a lot of people.

00:08:32.578 --> 00:08:34.780
-Yeah, that's true.
That was the moment.

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:35.981
-And I actually
kind of needed them

00:08:35.981 --> 00:08:37.049
because this tooth
was a little...

00:08:37.049 --> 00:08:38.217
-So they
were actually like...

00:08:38.217 --> 00:08:39.986
Not that I would have run out
and be like,

00:08:39.986 --> 00:08:41.320
Oh, I'm going to get braces.

00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:42.488
-So it's actually a nice cover

00:08:42.488 --> 00:08:43.889
that you got to do it
in a movie.

00:08:43.889 --> 00:08:45.090
-Yeah.
-And then everybody afterward...

00:08:45.090 --> 00:08:46.759
-It all works out.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:08:46.759 --> 00:08:48.527
-And then you were like,
"You're great in that movie.

00:08:48.527 --> 00:08:50.429
And also your teeth
look fantastic."

00:08:50.429 --> 00:08:52.831
-Yes.
Thank you.

00:08:52.831 --> 00:08:54.400
-Congrats on the film and thanks
so much for being here.

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:56.435
-Oh, it was great being here.
-You're such a delight.

00:08:56.435 --> 00:08:57.970
- Guys. That's Parker Posey.

00:08:57.970 --> 00:08:59.805
"Beau is Afraid" is in
theaters now.

00:08:59.805 --> 00:09:01.507
We'll be right back with more
"Late Night."

